From 2f5d6e8ac68877ed64f06200afd7f6c8c585de68 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: yankneck696 <yankneck696@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 11:24:52 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 4/9] PureSignal: take out what is left of the LO difference,
 and pin the table at the highest amplitude sent (#525)

Two things found while chasing a table that sank to the floor on a
PlutoSky R2. Neither turned out to be what sank it there (that was the
update itself, in the next commit), but both are real.

The loop gain was fitted as one complex constant per block. If the two
synthesisers are not exactly the spun-out offset apart, the loop gain turns
inside the block; the constant fitted to that comes out too small, each
entry's own estimate scatters in phase around it, and phase scatter reads
as gain the amplifier has too much of, so every entry is pulled down. In a
simulation of a two-tone over at 2.5 Msps, 300 Hz of residual takes every
learned entry to -12 dB within a second.

- The rotation is now measured from how the loop gain turns between
  1024-sample slices of the block and taken out before anything is fitted.
  It is exposed as residual_hz(), so a figure that stays large says the
  offset given to feed_back() is wrong. On the PlutoSky R2 it reads 0-10 Hz,
  so this is protection for radios whose synthesisers sit further apart.
- The table was normalised at its top entry whether or not anything reached
  it. Below full scale that left the learned part's level free, and let the
  highest entry actually sent rise above unity (+1.2 dB in the same
  simulation with a compressing amplifier), which the module promises
  cannot happen. It is now normalised at the highest entry the feedback has
  reached; entries above that hold unity.

This is the shared predistorter, so the LimeSDR-USB's PureSignal gets both.
A new test runs that two-tone over with 300 Hz of residual and checks that
the residual is measured, that the table stays within 6 dB, and that no
entry asks for more than unity. It fails on the old code on both counts.
The existing tests, including linearising a compressing amplifier, pass
unchanged.
