From 53e323b7891bae45578cbcfcf856c301be6662e6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: yankneck696 <yankneck696@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 13:58:31 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 6/9] PureSignal: learn only from blocks that line up well, and
 distrust an impossible LO residual (#525)

A transmit-level sweep on a PlutoSky R2 (two-tone, RX2 at 0 dB, a 46 dB
tap) learned smooth, believable curves from -20 dB to -6 dB of TX gain:
flat at -20 and -15, then -0.7, -1.5 and -2.0 dB of compression at -10, -7
and -6, which is where the board's own amplifier starts to run out. At
-2 dB and above the table scattered between the floor and the ceiling.
Two things were feeding it:

- Blocks after one of that link's 300 ms stalls read a leftover LO
  difference of -1112 Hz, and on a later over 231 Hz, against the 0-10 Hz
  it reads otherwise, and were rotated by it before learning. The residual
  is now only believed up to 50 Hz; a block reading more is misaligned, and
  is not learned from.
- Locked, the alignment was confirmed every half second. Each stall moves
  the delay at once, and half a second of blocks learned at the old
  alignment put a 12 dB bump in the middle of the table, which made the
  two-tone go wide. The near search (well under a millisecond) now runs on
  every block while locked, so a jump is caught before the block is used.
- Blocks were learned from as long as the lock held, at an envelope match
  of 0.5. A block that only just matches is usually one whose alignment
  has slipped (each stall moves the delay), and a driven-hard amplifier
  matches less well to begin with. The table now learns only from blocks
  matching at 0.85 or better; the lock itself still holds down to 0.5.
  Held alignments on the R2 match at 0.9 and up; with RX2's gain raised to
  10 or 20 dB, which overloads it with that tap, the match fell to 0.7-0.8
  and the table scattered the same way.

A new test feeds back a two-tone 400 Hz further from the spun-out offset
than it should be, and checks that the residual is read and the table is
left alone; it fails without the limit. The two tests from the previous
commits are retuned so they still test something with these limits: the
residual test runs at 40 Hz, inside the limit, for longer, and now also
checks that something was learned; and the weak
