From a3ec89f9273333a16b1d1324bff0088dcd6eceb2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: yankneck696 <yankneck696@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 16:56:13 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 7/9] PureSignal: measure the amplifier and work the table out
 from it, a cycle at a time (#525)

On a PlutoSky R2 at -6 dB of TX gain the table learned a smooth -1.8 dB
curve for about twenty seconds, then started to move, and the two-tone
quivered and spread. No stalls, dropped samples or misalignment in that
stretch: the loop was oscillating. A simulation with the radio's loop delay
of about 0.18 s reproduces it, the table swinging +4, -12, +5 dB and
growing, and with a 0.05 s loop it settles.

Each block nudged the table towards its own inverse, judged from feedback
that, on a networked radio, describes the table as it was dozens of updates
ago. Correcting today's entry by that stale error corrects the same error
over and over. The learning is now done the way WDSP's PureSignal (Warren
Pratt, NR0V, calcc.c in the OpenHPSDR wdsp sources) does it, written afresh
here rather than ported:

- The gain each sample was sent with is kept beside the reference, and the
  feedback is used to measure the amplifier on its own: for each level that
  went in (wanted sample times sent gain), what came out. That curve does
  not depend on the table in force. It is averaged over blocks, each block
  first fitted to the curve already held so that only its shape is kept
  (the overall loop gain divided out moves with the table).
- The table is worked out from the curve: for each wanted amplitude, the
  drive that makes the amplifier put out a scaled copy of it, scaled so the
  highest level measured goes through at unity, the AM/PM turned back too.
- In cycles: eight feedback blocks are measured at one table, the table is
  rebuilt, and the next cycle measures only samples sent since. WDSP waits
  out a set loop delay after each correction; here the delay is known from
  the alignment, so the wait is exact.

Measuring forwards (output against what went in) rather than backwards
keeps the weak-feedback fix: the input side carries no receiver noise, so
binning by it is unbiased, where binning by the noisy output dragged the
low levels to the floor again.

In simulation with the default rate, the table settles on the same smooth
curve with a 0.05, 0.18 or 0.4 s loop, and with an amplifier compressed
hard enough to need 3-5 dB; only one driven past the 12 dB the table may
correct still wanders, as nothing can fix that. A new test runs a two-tone
through a compressing amplifier with a 0.2 s loop and checks the table
settles; the previous update oscillates until it loses lock. The other
tests pass unchanged.
---
